    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Hyundai Tucson N Line Steals the Spotlight in Paris, but It's All Show and No Go\nToyota Asks Europe To Change Which Vehicles Count as Zero-Emission and Locally Made\n8 photos\nPhoto: Toyota\nThe world's largest automaker wants an internal combustion vehicle to qualify as zero-emission in Europe, provided it runs only on approved renewable fuels. That is the single most important request buried in the company's latest policy paper, ahead of all the requests that touch upon competitiveness and other topics. Toyota, in other words, is asking EU legislators to change how they count the vehicles manufacturers can sell in the European space.\nThe proposed VEEF category includes vehicles running exclusively on eligible fuels, not internal combustion vehicles whose owners choose a renewable blend once in a blue moon. Toyota wants one EU definition tied to the Renewable Energy Directive, namely RED III. Zero-emission would describe the vehicle's regulatory treatment under said proposal.\nThat request accompanies a push for a 90 percent reduction in new vehicle CO2 emissions by 2025 rather than 100 percent. Toyota wants renewable fuels and green steel to help close the remaining gap. However, it doesn't say that 10 percent of sales should automatically go to combustion vehicles. A fleet emissions reduction and a powertrain sales quota are different animals in the grand scheme of things.\nToyota is asking for more than a different endgame. Per the attached release, the automaker wants a five-year compliance window from 2028 to 2032 to smooth the transition through 2030 and 2031. A weak electric sales year could then sit alongside a stronger one instead of demanding that the factory plan, dealer allocations, and customer demand all fall into step at precisely the same moment.\nAs you might have already guessed, the company's case clearly rests on a forecast rather than a certainty. Toyota puts battery-electric vehicles at roughly 45 percent of new vehicle demand in 2030, whereas current rules mandate more than 55 percent. The gap is understandably wider for commercial vehicles.\nPhoto: Toyota The actual EU rule controls average CO2 emissions rather than a BEV quota. Toyota's argument is that the expected buying preferences and the emissions match don't line up. There is, however, a pretty evident commercial interest here. Toyota claims that manufacturers should focus their R&D budgets on technology rather than compliance, and I can fully understand where they're coming from.\nMore flexibility would also let automakers operating in the European bloc to manage their product mix with fewer dramatic adjustments when electric demand misses expectations. That's why Toyota asks for regular reviews if demand and infrastructure develop too slowly. It simply wants the timetable to follow the market far more closely than ever before.\nBritain gets its own request, specifically maximum required zero-emission shares of 50 percent for cars and 40 percent for light commercial vehicles in 2030. Toyota wants those compliance options to continue through 2035, with flexibilities, mirroring the five-year approach for the EU. A completed mandate revision in 2027 is also mentioned.\nLast but not least, Toyota's proposed 'Made with Europe' approach would recognize a limited group of customs-union and free-trade reciprocal partners. The press release attached below explicitly names Britain, Turkey, and Japan. The request concerns the Industrial Accelerator Act and eligibility across incentives, public procurement, and corporate fleet.\nToyota has invested in excess of 12 billion euros in Europe since 1990, and as of today, it directly employs more than 26,000 people. Toyota further proposes phasing European content up to 70 percent after three years, measured against ex-works vehicle value. Separately, it seeks a 70 percent overall vehicle sales threshold rather than the proposed 85 percent.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEurope Toyota Japan UK Turkey\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ford Recalls Certain F-150 Trucks Over Front Seats That May Not Be Bolted Down Properly",
